# Animals first left alone, then given rules.
# moobloom, ferret, shima enaga, parrot, jungle scorpion.
#
# Row format:   group = spring summer autumn winter     (ordinary biomes)
#               group = dry wet                         (tropical biomes)
# The first row that fits wins. 'any' fits everything.
#
# A chance can only LOWER a spawn rate. So "a spring boost" is written as
# the other seasons being cut. The peak season stays at 1.0.

[moobloom]
animals    = friendsandfoes:moobloom
confidence = guess
source     = https://sicb.org/?p=11571
             https://complete.bioone.org/journals/northwest-science/volume-97/issue-3/046.097.0304/Flower-Phenology-Bumble-Bee-Foraging-and-Climate-Change-in-North/10.3955/046.097.0304.full
why        = The moobloom is a fantasy cow that lives on flowers. It spawns in flower
             forest, sunflower plains, meadow and cherry grove. There is no real animal
             to copy, so this follows the flowers. Meadow studies show the bloom rising
             in spring and peaking in spring and early summer. Pollinators track it. The
             sources do not measure the autumn drop, so autumn and winter are a judgement
             call: some flowers last into autumn, and in winter there are almost none.
             Spring and summer are full. Autumn is a little over half. Winter is a quarter.
#                 spring summer autumn winter
any = 1.0  1.0  0.55 0.25

[ferret]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:ferret
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://vwt.org.uk/species/polecat/
             https://www.vwt.org.uk/news-media/news/national-polecat-survey-update
why        = The ferret is a tame polecat, so this reads the wild European polecat.
             Polecats mate in February to March and the kits are born in early summer.
             Kits leave their mother and spread out in autumn, so September has many
             young animals on the move. In winter polecats are less active and are seen
             much less. Live sightings peak in summer, when they are more active in the
             day. Winter is cut to about half. Spring is a little thin, since the females
             are in dens.
#                 spring summer autumn winter
any = 0.8  1.0  1.0  0.5

[shima-enaga]
animals    = crittersandcompanions:shima_enaga
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://bharasymiw1.photobiz.com/blog/hokkaido-bird-watching-photography-workshop-shima-enaga-flocking-behavior
             https://www.fun-japan.jp/en/articles/14237
why        = The Hokkaido bird stays all year, so it never leaves. What changes is how
             easy it is to find. In winter, family flocks of 10 to 20 roost and move
             together, and they defend a winter ground. In spring (about April) the flocks
             break up into pairs and the birds are spread thin and hidden while nesting.
             So spring and summer are lower and autumn and winter are full. Both sources
             are tour and travel pages, not field studies. The drop is small.
#                 spring summer autumn winter
any = 0.7  0.7  1.0  1.0

[parrot]
animals    = minecraft:parrot
confidence = guess
source     = https://www.audubon.org/magazine/tropical-bird-breeding-seasons-explained
             https://sora.unm.edu/sites/default/files/journals/wilson/v111n04/p0488-p0493.pdf
why        = Tropical parrots do not follow four seasons. Some species nest in the wet
             season (scarlet macaws, January to April in Peru). A study of green-rumped
             parrotlets in Venezuela found breeding stops in the dry season and flocks of
             over 100 form. So in the wet half the pairs are in tree holes and fewer are
             in the open, and in the dry half the flocks are out in the open. Other
             parrots do the opposite, and the sources cover only a few species. This is
             a small cut, not a rule about all parrots.
#                  dry  wet
any = 1.0  0.8

[jungle-scorpion]
animals    = naturalist:jungle_scorpion
confidence = guess
source     = none found
why        = I found no source on jungle scorpion seasons. This is general reasoning.
             Scorpions are cold-blooded and wet-forest invertebrates tend to be most
             active when it is warm and humid. In the dry half of a tropical year the
             leaf litter dries out and they hide deeper. So the dry half is cut a
             little. This is a guess. Change it or delete the section if you disagree.
#                  dry  wet
any = 0.7  1.0
